00:45:07.420 --> 00:45:11.000
<v Juan Pablo Galavis>I like you a lot. A lot. So, um--

00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:26.990
<v Taylor Rose Nations>So I kept joking that if my Bachelor application fell through, at least I'd have this to fall back on. Which was kind of funny, because my Bachelor application did fall through, and-- and here I am.

00:45:26.990 --> 00:45:49.320
<v Taylor Rose Nations>They had said by the end of December 2013, we'll hear something. That week comes, I realize I hadn't heard anything. I want to hear something soon. And I woke up, and I always check my email in the morning on my phone. And I got that letter, and like, did the whole eye-rubbing, like, "No way!" thing.

00:45:49.320 --> 00:46:10.510
<v Taylor Rose Nations>"Dear Miss Taylor Rose Nations, you and only 1,057 others around the globe have been preselected as potential candidates to launch the dawn of a new era, human life on Mars. Congratulations, you have made it to the next round. Now catch your breath."

00:46:10.510 --> 00:46:23.140
<v Electronic British Female Voice>Mars One will establish human settlement on Mars in 2023. In that year, the first group of four humans will land on Mars.

00:46:23.140 --> 00:46:27.970
<v Woman>The Dutch organization Mars One hopes to build a community of settlers on the planet.

00:46:27.970 --> 00:46:31.830
<v Electronic British Female Voice>Every two years after that, another group will join the settlement.

00:46:31.830 --> 00:46:34.860
<v Man>It's an idea belonging to Bas Lansdorp.

00:46:34.860 --> 00:46:35.740
<v Man>[APPLAUSE]

00:46:35.740 --> 00:46:42.660
<v Bas Lansdorp>I am Bas Lansdorp, and I am organizing a human mission to the planet Mars in the year 2023.

00:46:42.660 --> 00:47:05.130
<v Electronic British Female Voice>Mars One has designed a mission that is much simpler than previous designs for Mars missions. The most significant simplification is that the crew is actually going to stay and live on Mars with the intention to remain there for the rest of their lives.

00:47:05.130 --> 00:47:27.710
<v Taylor Rose Nations>Jumped out of bed. My roommate was down the hall, and I was knocking on her door, freaking out. I'm like Megan! I'm going to Mars! She said, "That's [BLEEP] awesome!"

00:47:27.710 --> 00:47:42.040
<v Taylor Rose Nations>I'm, like, just sitting in the living room, thinking, like, this could actually happen. I'm just rereading that letter over and over and realizing that I didn't feel-- I didn't feel scared about it. I wasn't worried.

00:47:42.040 --> 00:48:09.160
<v Taylor Rose Nations>When I told my dad, I was in Whistler, and he had been watching Game of Thrones , which I got him for Christmas. I told him I had been accepted and that there were 1,000 of us left. It was silent on the phone for like 30 seconds. He got all teary, and said, "Aw, you're going to Mars, and Ned Stark's getting his head chopped off. What's next?"

00:48:09.160 --> 00:48:26.300
<v Taylor Rose Nations>The one-way mission means we don't ever get to come home. But we're going to be setting up camp there. A lot of people have been talking to me, like, is this a scam? You had to pay $35 to apply. Do they just want your money?

00:48:26.300 --> 00:48:37.940
<v Taylor Rose Nations>"Don't go! You're just a human guinea pig." I don't mind. If I can go to Mars and be a human guinea pig, I'm willing to sort of donate my body to science.

00:48:37.940 --> 00:49:05.020
<v Taylor Rose Nations>I feel like it's worth it for me, personally, and it's kind of a selfish thing, but just to turn around and look and see Earth, like that's a lifelong, like, total dream. Never thought in a million years that that would be something I could ever experience. That, to me, is worth the whole thing, worth, like, my spaceship crashing or getting taken advantage of. To me, that in itself is worth it.

00:49:05.020 --> 00:49:11.860
<v Taylor Rose Nations>Oh, you know what? They did have a handicapped girl. They had a chick-- whoa! They have a pregnant one?

00:49:11.860 --> 00:49:14.310
<v Elizabeth Jenkins>And so this is the season that you applied for, right?

00:49:14.310 --> 00:49:19.400
<v Taylor Rose Nations>Yeah. I sent out my application for The Bachelor and Mars One around the same time.

00:49:19.400 --> 00:49:23.800
<v Juan Pablo Galavis>I take a step back and I see them, and I'm like, wow, you're lucky, man.

00:49:23.800 --> 00:49:33.590
<v Taylor Rose Nations>All the girls, they have, like, 30 seconds to sell themselves to this dude when they walk out of the limo. Some of them tumble out and do gymnastics, and others have prepared jokes.

00:49:33.590 --> 00:49:35.240
<v Chris Harrison>Hi, I'm Chris Harrison.

00:49:35.240 --> 00:49:36.890
<v Taylor Rose Nations>Oh. Here he is. Love that guy.

00:49:36.890 --> 00:49:38.730
<v Chris Harrison>Welcome to the new season of The Bachelor .

00:49:38.730 --> 00:49:48.500
<v Elizabeth Jenkins>And how do you think that's going to be, going from a view of reality TV to being the person that could potentially be in a lifelong reality TV show?

00:49:48.500 --> 00:50:12.630
<v Taylor Rose Nations>Unlike The Bachelor , where you're on a show and it's a season, and you go and you vacation and make out with Juan Pablo, like, if you actually get selected for Mars One, it's the rest of your life. So it's probably really important to be completely authentic. That way you're not selected on false pretenses, and-- and then you end up hating everything. [LAUGHS] And, yeah.

00:50:12.630 --> 00:50:14.660
<v Elizabeth Jenkins>And you're going to be there with only three other people, right?

00:50:14.660 --> 00:50:16.280
<v Taylor Rose Nations>Mhm. That's the plan.

00:50:16.280 --> 00:50:21.630
<v Elizabeth Jenkins>Do you ever think to yourself, like, that's not a lot of options for potential sexual partners?

00:50:21.630 --> 00:50:51.170
<v Taylor Rose Nations>[LAUGHS] Mm, yes. But I also think at least initially, we will basically just be trying to survive. And so we probably won't be-- well, I don't know. I don't know. Yeah, I've thought about that a little bit. But also, four more people are coming in two years, so that's like seven people. [LAUGHS] Water, food, a good book, and a vibrator-- you're set. [LAUGHS]

00:50:51.170 --> 00:51:11.060
<v Taylor Rose Nations>So I had submitted my application to Mars One probably a few months prior to submitting my video. You don't have to do it all at once. So we developed a little script about me in the future, when Earth's gone to [BLEEP]. And it's really dorky and it's awful. And hope nobody's ever seen it or will ever watch it.

00:51:11.060 --> 00:51:23.210
<v Taylor Rose Nations>My name is Taylor. The date is March 10, 2085. And I'm here in America. Things on Earth are getting worse every day.

00:51:23.210 --> 00:51:41.100
<v Taylor Rose Nations>With the rising sea levels, we're all forced to live so close to each other, and disease is spreading rampantly. We've got no food. We've got no drinking water left. What is available as being hoarded by those with money. People are dying in the streets of dehydration and starvation.

00:51:41.100 --> 00:52:00.350
<v Taylor Rose Nations>The hurricanes are coming almost constantly now, and there's no escaping them. I fear that the end is near. Why did we not do something about this while we had the chance? Why did we not expand beyond Earth? Why did we not go to Mars?

00:52:00.350 --> 00:52:00.860
<v Man>Status Buster?

00:52:00.860 --> 00:52:01.550
<v Man>Go.

00:52:01.550 --> 00:52:02.420
<v Man>Chancey?

00:52:02.420 --> 00:52:02.860
<v Man>Go.

00:52:02.860 --> 00:52:05.410
<v Man>Go ahead and throttle up, CAPCOM. Fido?

00:52:05.410 --> 00:52:06.190
<v Man>Go.

00:52:06.190 --> 00:52:06.610
<v Man>Buster?

00:52:06.610 --> 00:52:07.720
<v Man>Go.

00:52:07.720 --> 00:52:16.870
<v Man>[VOICES CONTINUE  AND FADE]

00:52:16.870 --> 00:52:55.260
<v Taylor Rose Nations>It's funny how much added value everyday experiences kind of have when you know that you-- in 10 years, you might not have any of those anymore. I guess it's kind of like if you know when you're going to die. Like, your know when your day on Earth is. All of your days up to that day will be so much more important, and you'll experience them so much more than if you were just going on like everybody else. Especially at night, when it's windy and dry, I'm just like, I only have a set amount of these left.

00:52:55.260 --> 00:53:02.470
<v Arno Wielders>This is extraordinary project. No taxpayers' money will be involved.

00:53:02.470 --> 00:53:06.900
<v Arno Wielders>We will finance this mission by creating the biggest media event ever around.

00:53:06.900 --> 00:53:10.070
<v Woman>It's a Big Brother -style competition.

00:53:10.070 --> 00:53:16.680
<v Arno Wielders>This is going to be a media spectacle. Big Brother will pale in comparison.

00:53:16.680 --> 00:53:27.090
<v Arno Wielders>So everybody can watch as we progress, the astronauts are selected, as they go into training, and as they explore Mars.

00:53:27.090 --> 00:53:35.350
<v Arno Wielders>The whole world will be watching and experience this journey.

00:53:35.350 --> 00:53:46.180
<v Taylor Rose Nations>I kind of feel like 1,058 people just volunteered for a tribute, like The Hunger Games . And we're all just kind of like standing around, wondering what's going to happen.

00:53:46.180 --> 00:53:53.370
<v Taylor Rose Nations>When I picture the launch, I picture a lot of media. From that point forward, our entire lives will be televised.

00:53:53.370 --> 00:54:09.960
<v Taylor Rose Nations>I think the lead-up to takeoff is going to be harder than post-takeoff, because it's, like, all this attention, and-- and you have to kind of be a public figure. Nobody knows who I am right now, but 10 years from now, people will, if things go as planned.

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:13.000
<v Elizabeth Jenkins>But it seems like you're-- like that would definitely be attention on you.

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:13.210
<v Taylor Rose Nations>Mhm.

00:54:13.210 --> 00:54:16.790
<v Elizabeth Jenkins>You know, how do you think you're going to handle that as someone who's sort of nervous?

00:54:16.790 --> 00:54:30.180
<v Taylor Rose Nations>I think it's getting easier. I feel like every year, I sort of feel a little bit more comfortable in my own skin, if that makes sense. I still do the whole awkward robot thing, but now I just embrace it. [LAUGHS]

00:54:30.180 --> 00:54:50.310
<v Taylor Rose Nations>So it's gotten less-- it's still hard. But I feel like I've been purposely putting myself out there a lot more than I have. I'm trying to put myself in social situations where I don't know people, and like, I have to learn to talk to people. Or, like, I've been going on dates a little bit.

00:54:50.310 --> 00:55:03.670
<v Taylor Rose Nations>But I'm not at all in a rush to commit to anything. Not looking for long-term relationships. I've got 10 years, people.

00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:25.270
<v Taylor Rose Nations>You know, I think about it a lot, that I only have one life to live and I can choose to do whatever I want with it. So I can choose to stay in Olympia and hang out here and play roller derby and ride my motorcycle and live life that way. Or if I choose to go to medical school, that'll be another path. And so given the choice, I feel like it's kind of an easy decision.

00:55:25.270 --> 00:55:32.270
<v Elizabeth Jenkins>If you do get to that next round, it's for keeps. You know, you're not going to be able to leave the planet. What if you get there and it sucks?

00:55:32.270 --> 00:55:52.530
<v Taylor Rose Nations>Yeah, that-- that would suck.  It probably will suck pretty-- like, it will suck, I'm sure. But you-- you get to know that your life is about something bigger than just, like, paying your bills and living and dying and being boring.

00:55:52.530 --> 00:56:28.670
<v Ira Glass>That story was produced by Elizabeth Jenkins for the podcast Love + Radio -- which, I have to say, has some of the most emotionally complicated stories and portraits of people that anybody is doing anywhere. It's on the Radiotopia network. I totally recommend it. This story was edited and mixed by Nick van der Kolk and Brendan Baker. You can find Love + Radio at loveandradio.org.

